      "message": "yeah, we have this corner case with 0x in the middle.\nbut if we just want a sequence of bytes, why do we even break it into 3 integers here (and then do a confusing htole32 before sending).\nI\u0027d rather just have\n  #define SN_BITS_SIZE (3*4)\n  uint8_t sn_bits[SN_BITS_SIZE];\nand make parse_sn_bits() and process_sn_bits() work with it.\n\nstatic void process_sn_bits(...) {\n  ...\n  memcpy(command_body, sn_bits, SN_BITS_SIZE);\n  ...\n}\n\nstatic int parse_sn_bits(const char *opt, uint8_t *sn_bits)\n{\n  if (strlen(opt) !\u003d SN_BITS_SIZE * 2))\n    return 0;\n  for (int i \u003d 0; i \u003c SN_BITS_SIZE; i++) {\n    if (!parse_hex_byte(opt + 2*i, sn_bits + i))\n      return 0;\n  }\n  return 1;\n}\n\nwhere parse_hex_byte() either uses smth like sscanf(\"%2x\", ...) or does manual parsing. well, you know the drill:\nint parse_hex_byte(char* src, uint8_t* raw_byte) {\n  *raw_byte \u003d 0;\n  for (int i \u003d 0; i \u003c 2; i++, *raw_byte \u003c\u003c\u003d 4) {\n    if (src[i] \u003e\u003d \u00270\u0027 \u0026\u0026 src[i] \u003c\u003d \u00279\u0027) {\n      *raw_byte +\u003d src[i] - \u00270\u0027;\n  ...\n    } else {\n      return 0;\n    }\n  }\n  return 1;\n}",
